---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Header from '../../components/Header.astro';
import Footer from '../../components/Footer.astro';
import { siteContent } from '../../data/siteContent';

const note = siteContent.notes[0];

const noteArticleSchema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Article',
  headline: `${note.title}: ${note.subtitle}`,
  description: note.summary,
  datePublished: '2026-07-01T00:00:00Z',
  author: {
    '@type': 'Person',
    name: siteContent.meta.name,
    url: siteContent.meta.url,
  },
  publisher: {
    '@type': 'Person',
    name: siteContent.meta.name,
  },
  keywords: note.tags.join(', '),
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title={`${note.title} — ${siteContent.meta.name}`}
  description={note.summary}
  canonicalPath="/notes/flexible-but-not-free"
>
  <Header />

  <main id="main-content" class="flex-1 w-full max-w-[960px] mx-auto px-6 sm:px-8 py-12 sm:py-20">
    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-12 gap-8 md:gap-12 items-start">
      <!-- Left Column: Back link & Date -->
      <aside class="md:col-span-3 md:sticky md:top-24 space-y-6 text-xs font-mono-spec text-[var(--text-muted)]">
        <a
          href="/#notes"
          class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 text-[var(--text-secondary)] hover:text-[var(--text-primary)] transition-colors"
        >
          ← Back to home
        </a>

        <div class="space-y-1 pt-2 border-t border-[var(--rule-hairline)]">
          <div class="text-[var(--text-primary)] font-medium">{note.date}</div>
          <div>{note.readTime}</div>
        </div>
      </aside>

      <!-- Right Column: Essay Content -->
      <article class="md:col-span-9 max-w-[650px]">
        <header class="pb-8 mb-8 border-b border-[var(--rule-hairline)]">
          <h1 class="font-display text-3xl sm:text-4xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-[var(--text-primary)] leading-tight">
            {note.title}
          </h1>
          <p class="text-lg text-[var(--text-secondary)] mt-2.5 leading-snug">
            {note.subtitle}
          </p>
        </header>

        <div class="space-y-6 text-base sm:text-[16.5px] text-[var(--text-secondary)] leading-[1.8] font-sans">
          <p class="text-[var(--text-primary)] font-medium">
            Every gig platform promises the same bargain: work whenever you want, earn on your own schedule, and keep complete freedom over your week. When you are studying full-time in a city as expensive as Toronto, that pitch sounds like the ideal arrangement.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you have a networking lab at George Brown on Monday morning, a routing assignment due Tuesday night, and an exam on Friday, traditional shift work is hard to fit around classes. App-based delivery or gig work seems to solve the problem by letting you log on and off whenever you have a spare block of hours.
          </p>

          <p>
            Over time, though, you notice that this flexibility isn't actually free. The cost just shows up somewhere else — mostly in fragmented attention, physical wear, and the quiet erosion of deep study time.
          </p>

          <h2 class="font-display text-xl sm:text-2xl font-semibold text-[var(--text-primary)] pt-4">
            The illusion of interchangeable hours
          </h2>

          <p>
            On paper, three separate one-hour gaps look the same as a single three-hour block. In practice, they aren't even close. Studying subnetting, troubleshooting a broken Linux DHCP config, or building a multi-VM Active Directory lab requires uninterrupted concentration just to load the context into your head.
          </p>

          <p>
            When your day is split between traffic, notifications, and short shifts, your mind stays in a constant state of context-switching. Even after you park and sit back down at your desk, it takes half an hour before you're actually thinking clearly again.
          </p>

          <h2 class="font-display text-xl sm:text-2xl font-semibold text-[var(--text-primary)] pt-4">
            Hidden operating costs
          </h2>

          <p>
            The financial math can be just as misleading if you only look at gross payouts. Fuel, maintenance, tires, insurance, and depreciation happen gradually in the background, so you rarely feel them on the same day you get paid.
          </p>

          <p>
            More importantly, there is the opportunity cost: every exhausted evening spent recovering from fragmented shifts is an evening not spent building real lab experience, finishing a certification like Network+, or working on projects that move your career forward permanently.
          </p>

          <h2 class="font-display text-xl sm:text-2xl font-semibold text-[var(--text-primary)] pt-4">
            Protecting time for the work that compounds
          </h2>

          <p>
            Living and studying in Toronto requires being practical — bills have to be paid. But the biggest shift for me was realizing that study blocks and hands-on lab time need to be guarded with the same seriousness as a scheduled job.
          </p>

          <p>
            Batching work into predictable days rather than letting it bleed into every free hour makes all the difference. Real flexibility doesn't come from an app letting you choose when to go online; it comes from building skills that are solid enough to give you real options over the long run.
          </p>
        </div>

        <footer class="mt-14 pt-8 border-t border-[var(--rule-hairline)] flex items-center justify-between text-xs font-mono-spec text-[var(--text-muted)]">
          <span>Tarik Gungor · Toronto, ON</span>
          <a href="/#notes" class="text-[var(--text-primary)] hover:underline">
            ← All notes
          </a>
        </footer>
      </article>
    </div>
  </main>

  <Footer />

  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(noteArticleSchema)} />
</BaseLayout>
